
\ A handler receives the message on top of the restored surrounding stack.
[ "intentional error" error ] [] try "intentional error" == check
[ 1 0 / ] [] try "divide by zero" contains? true == check
[ missing-word ] [] try "undefined word 'missing-word'" == check
0 [ "intentional error" error ] [ drop 1 + ] try 1 == check
0 [ 1 0 / ] [ drop 1 + ] try 1 == check

\ `dip` does not need a private rollback snapshot. An enclosing `try` restores
\ both the exposed stack and the value hidden by `dip`.
[] 10 [ [ 1 push-back "dip error" error ] dip ] [] try
"dip error" == check
10 == check
[] == check

\ Recovery inside the body completes normally and `dip` restores its value.
0 10 [ [ 1 0 / ] [ drop 1 + ] try ] dip
10 == check
1 == check

\ `keep` has the same threading behavior for values beneath its input.
[] 10 [ [ drop 1 push-back "keep error" error ] keep ] [] try
"keep error" == check
10 == check
[] == check

0 10 [ [ 1 0 / ] [ drop 1 + ] try ] keep
11 == check
10 == check
0 == check

\ `fold` threads both its accumulator and the ambient stack without retaining
\ ambient collections solely for error cleanup.
[] [ 0 [ 0 ] [ | accumulator item |
        1 push-back
        "fold error" error
    ] fold ] [] try
"fold error" == check
[] == check

[] 0 [ 0 ] [
    [ "inner fold error" error ] [ drop + ] try
] fold
0 == check
[] == check

\ `bi` threads its ambient stack between branches. Only the duplicated input is
\ intentionally retained.
[] 10 [
    [ drop 1 push-back "bi error" error ] [] bi
] [] try
"bi error" == check
10 == check
[] == check

0 10 [ [ 1 0 / ] [ drop 1 + ] try ] [] bi
10 == check
11 == check
0 == check

\ `cond` retains its observer snapshot while selecting a clause, then releases
\ it before the selected body updates the restored stack.
[] [ [ [ true ] [ 1 push-back ] ] ] cond [ 1 ] == check

[] [
    [ [ [ true ] [ 1 push-back "cond error" error ] ] ] cond
] [] try
"cond error" == check
[] == check

\ `binrec` retains its hidden second branch, but not the threaded stack solely
\ to reconstruct an error that an enclosing `try` already restores.
[] [
    1 [ 0 == ] [ "binrec error" error ] [ pred dup ] [ + ] binrec
] [] try
"binrec error" == check
[] == check

1
[ 0 == ]
[ [ "inner binrec error" error ] [ drop drop 1 ] try ]
[ pred dup ]
[ + ] binrec
2 == check

\ An inner handler can recover without invoking the outer handler.
0 [
    [ "inner error" error ] [ drop 1 + ] try
    1 +
] [ drop 100 + ] try
2 == check

\ A handler error propagates to the next enclosing handler.
0 [
    [ "inner error" error ] [ drop "propagated error" error ] try
] [ drop 10 + ] try
10 == check

[
    [ "inner" error ] [ drop "outer" error ] try
] [] try
"outer" == check
